WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.449 --> 00:00:11.785
&gt;&gt; Beach Goers, beware.

00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:13.453
A stretch of seaweed, 5,000

00:00:13.453 --> 00:00:14.254
miles wide, about twice the

00:00:14.387 --> 00:00:18.458
width of the United States is

00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:19.459
drifting towards Florida's

00:00:19.459 --> 00:00:19.593
coast.

00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:24.431
And it's a seaweed variety

00:00:24.564 --> 00:00:26.066
called sargasm that forms large

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:26.399
blooms.

00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:31.471
It could be the largest on

00:00:31.471 --> 00:00:31.571
record.

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:33.807
I'm joined now by Dr. Tracy

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:34.140
fenara.

00:00:34.274 --> 00:00:36.142
Good to see you.

00:00:36.276 --> 00:00:40.413
Where is this collection of

00:00:40.547 --> 00:00:41.715
clumps of seaweed?

00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:42.249
Where's it heading?

00:00:42.382 --> 00:00:44.451
&gt;&gt; We get a bloom every year.

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:48.455
It's been going on for hundreds

00:00:48.588 --> 00:00:49.489
of years.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:50.824
It comes from the north

00:00:50.957 --> 00:00:51.491
atlantic.

00:00:51.491 --> 00:00:52.359
It's in the caribbean and

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:53.560
eventually into the gulf.

00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:54.894
We don't know exactly where it

00:00:55.028 --> 00:00:56.363
will be in peak season and how

00:00:56.496 --> 00:00:59.566
much of it will come to our

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:02.302
shores and hit our beaches in

00:01:02.435 --> 00:01:03.169
southwest Florida.

00:01:03.503 --> 00:01:06.740
But this bloom has increased in

00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:07.040
size.

00:01:07.173 --> 00:01:07.674
Doubled from December to

00:01:07.807 --> 00:01:11.511
January and now is still

00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:12.512
growing.

00:01:12.646 --> 00:01:12.879
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:01:13.013 --> 00:01:13.513
So it sounds yucky.

00:01:13.546 --> 00:01:16.449
There's an upside to this,

00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:16.783
right?

00:01:16.916 --> 00:01:17.517
It does provide some shelter.

00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:22.856
Maybe even some food to a lot

00:01:22.989 --> 00:01:23.523
of marine life.

00:01:23.523 --> 00:01:25.525
So explain, I guess, how mother

00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:32.232
nature sees benefits of the

00:01:32.365 --> 00:01:32.632
sargassum.

00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:34.334
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:35.602
Sargassum patches are floating

00:01:35.735 --> 00:01:37.070
and moving ecosystem.

00:01:37.203 --> 00:01:38.705
They're essential for crabs and

00:01:38.838 --> 00:01:43.009
eels and important fish like

00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:43.610
amber jacks, jacks and

00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:43.977
mahimahi.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.146
It plays a huge role in our

00:01:46.279 --> 00:01:47.580
ecosystem.

00:01:47.714 --> 00:01:49.916
In addition to that, it's a

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.452
carbon mechanism as well.

00:01:52.585 --> 00:01:58.124
&gt;&gt; So then, are there also

00:01:58.258 --> 00:01:59.559
dangers with it?

00:01:59.559 --> 00:01:59.959
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:00.093 --> 00:02:02.629
Well, so it is nontoxic algae.

00:02:02.762 --> 00:02:03.563
It's a nontoxic species.

00:02:03.563 --> 00:02:05.765
When it comes to shore in mass

00:02:05.899 --> 00:02:07.567
amounts, that's when it can be

00:02:08.034 --> 00:02:09.169
a nuisance and be a problem.

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:19.045
So it can come to the shore,

00:02:19.179 --> 00:02:19.579
black out sunlight.

00:02:19.713 --> 00:02:22.315
Photo saint thinks can't take

00:02:22.449 --> 00:02:22.649
place.

00:02:22.782 --> 00:02:27.954
It can have areas with low to

00:02:28.088 --> 00:02:29.689
no oxygen further causing

00:02:29.823 --> 00:02:30.056
problems.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.692
It can suffocate coral reefs,

00:02:32.826 --> 00:02:33.593
interfere with turtle

00:02:33.693 --> 00:02:33.960
testerring.

00:02:34.094 --> 00:02:34.928
For beach Goers, it can be a

00:02:35.061 --> 00:02:40.433
gross day at the beach.

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.668
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:43.603
Because it's so fragrant.

00:02:43.603 --> 00:02:44.437
It's a strong smell.

00:02:44.571 --> 00:02:46.473
I guess with people for

00:02:46.606 --> 00:02:48.441
respiratory problems, it can be

00:02:48.575 --> 00:02:51.778
a real problem.

00:02:51.911 --> 00:02:52.212
Is that true?

00:02:52.345 --> 00:02:52.579
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:52.712 --> 00:02:54.714
Sulfur dioxide, one of the

00:02:54.848 --> 00:03:02.021
gases that is released from the

00:03:02.155 --> 00:03:03.022
decomposing sargassum smells

00:03:03.156 --> 00:03:04.457
like rotten eggs and can give

00:03:04.591 --> 00:03:05.825
you itchy eyes.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:08.428
What's the one two punch for

00:03:08.561 --> 00:03:14.734
southwest Florida is we have an

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.369
ongoing toxic species.

00:03:16.503 --> 00:03:19.105
They're known as Florida red

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:19.639
tide.

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:21.641
That bloom is already plaguing

00:03:21.741 --> 00:03:22.342
